{"author":{"id":"1832e4168d45d4ef31531146dbc743bb950a5dd543b15fd8e3a076fd976bfdef"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790976402,"nonce":"vFy4U8goYrucOd8C873wjf3SIDo=","title":"the wire cannot return a sequence of interfaces: GPUCompilationInfo.messages throws in the agent","message":"An attribute or operation whose result is a sequence (or FrozenArray) of interface objects encodes, in the generated agent, as\n\n    wire.reply(reply, 1, encode((w) =\u003e { w.seq(v, (x) =\u003e { throw new Error(\"objects come back under handles\"); }); }));\n\nso any non-empty result throws in the agent and the reply never carries it, and the guest encoder method (`gpu_compilation_info_get_messages(this, reply)`) has no handles to put the objects under. Found 2026-10-02 in WebGPU's GPUCompilationInfo.messages (opcode 119 in xgpu's browser wire), the only way a program on the web can read a shader's compilation messages. xgpu's web.rs needs it for its GpuCompilationInfo natives (compilation_count, compilation_message, compilation_type, compilation_line/column/offset/length); xgpu git-bug 106cf53fe7b624d802a6e53bffdb0a23e9ef7da8d3e4163cb6a461cb047b05ac.\n\nOne shape that fits the rest of the wire: the guest passes a first result handle and the agent stores item i under first + i, replying with the count. The guest reserves a range (xgpu's web.rs already makes transient handles below its plugin handles) and reads each item's attributes with the existing getters (gpu_compilation_message_get_message, _get_type, _get_line_num, ...).\n\nInterfaces with a sequence of dictionaries or primitives are unaffected; GPUSupportedFeatures.values already returns strings.","files":null}]}